WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.667
RLlib.

2
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:05.333
What do you do when reinforcement learning outgrows one machine?

3
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:10.667
RLlib is Ray's distributed reinforcement learning library.

4
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:15.033
Ray takes care of scheduling, fault tolerance, and moving data around.

5
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:20.300
It adds multi-agent environments, population-based training, and Ray Tune.

6
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.967
Here is the architecture.

7
00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:29.033
Many rollout workers simulate the environment, fanned out across the CPUs of a cluster.

8
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:35.367
They send their experience to a single learner, which does the gradient updates, usually on a GPU.

9
00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.500
Then fresh weights flow back out to the workers, and the loop continues.

10
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.033
Here is a PPO setup in RLlib.

11
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:44.700
We name the environment.

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.300
We ask for eight parallel rollout workers.

13
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.733
Their experience is pooled into large training batches.

14
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.136
Then we call train in a loop.

15
00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:55.456
The PPO math is the same as SB3.

16
00:00:55.456 --> 00:00:58.067
Only where rollouts run has changed.

17
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.567
So which one should you pick?

18
00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:04.012
SB3 has a small configuration surface.

19
00:01:04.012 --> 00:01:05.833
RLlib's is far larger.

20
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.035
SB3 handles one machine of simulators.

21
00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:12.700
RLlib shines with hundreds of parallel rollouts.

22
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
And RLlib means inheriting the operational weight of a distributed system.

23
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.233
The rule of thumb: SB3 until one machine is the bottleneck.

24
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:25.033
Knowing that line is the interview answer.

25
00:01:26.233 --> 00:01:28.067
Here is the classic situation.

26
00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:34.482
Your cubesat environment runs at three hundred steps a second, and PPO needs fifty million steps.

27
00:01:34.482 --> 00:01:36.500
That is two days on one machine.

28
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.333
So you point RLlib at your existing Kubernetes cluster, with two hundred environment runners.

29
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:44.936
The same training finishes over lunch.

30
00:01:44.936 --> 00:01:47.967
Not better math, just the rollout loop spread out.

31
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:50.024
Let's recap.

32
00:01:50.024 --> 00:01:53.667
RLlib is distributed reinforcement learning on Ray.

33
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:56.033
One learner, many rollout workers.

34
00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:00.767
The price is a bigger configuration and the weight of a distributed system.

35
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:06.086
So start with SB3, and move to RLlib when one machine is the limit.

36
00:02:06.086 --> 00:02:08.033
Next up: the ML lifecycle.
